Subject: quartz: present VMR7 video through a native child NSWindow on macOS
License: LGPL-2.1-or-later
Modification date: 2026-11-04

Ordinary GDI frames can be hidden beneath winemac's in-front OpenGL
surfaces or Metal content, leaving DirectShow OP video black.  A dedicated
native child window bypasses that ordering issue:  winemac.drv
gains a private versioned ExtEscape (include/wine/macdrv_video.h): the VMR7
allocator-presenter locks its read-only 32bpp surface, copies rows honouring
the signed pitch and hands packed BGRX frames to the driver, which builds an
sRGB CGImage and shows it in a borderless opaque child NSWindow scoped to the
presenter token, clipped/cropped to the parent content bounds.  RELEASE goes
through the cached root HWND and survives the video child's destruction; any
failure falls back to the previous StretchBlt path.  The video window is only
a canvas: it cannot take key/main status, ignores mouse events and is never
shown for a hidden or empty video HWND.

